  "solution": {
    "text": "Total energy requirement per molecule of <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub></mrow></math>:<br /><mtext><br />Binding energy <math> <math> <mrow> <mtext>=4</mtext><mtext>.526&nbsp;eV</mtext></mrow> </math> </math><br /><mtext></mtext><br /><math> <mrow> <mtext>I</mtext><mtext>.E</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>=</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>13</mtext><mtext>.60&nbsp;</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>eV</mtext></mrow> </math><br /><br />According to de Broglie's explanation of Bohr's orbit-</mtext><br /><br /><math> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mtext>2πr</mtext></mrow> <mtext>n</mtext> </msub> <mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>nλ</mtext></mrow> </math><br /><br />As per question, <math> <mrow> <mtext>2πr</mtext><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mtext>4λ</mtext></mrow> </math><br /><br />Thus, electron is excited to the 4<sup>th</sup> Bohr orbit<br /><br /><mtext> Excitation energy</mtext>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.85</mo><mo>−</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>.60</mo></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>.75</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>&nbsp;<br /><br />Thus, total eenergy requirement per molecule <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>.60</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>.75</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.526</mo><mo>≅</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>.87</mo><mi>eV</mi></math><br /><br />Thus, <math> <mtext>1</mtext> </math> molecule of <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mtext>2</mtext> </msub> </mrow> </math> will be dissociated by <math> <mtext>1</mtext> </math> atom of <math> <mtext>X</mtext> </math><br /><br />Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msub><mi>gH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>≡</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> molecules will be dissociated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> moles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math>."
  }
